# The Recommended rule of the same name holds every other intensifier.
# `genuinely` sits here, because it sometimes separates a real case
# from an apparent one (`a line is added only when the file genuinely
# ends in a blank line`). The adjective `genuine` stays out, because it
# usually means authentic (`the badge in the footer is genuine`).
#
# `very`, `really`, `truly`, and `actually` sit here too, because each
# hit 30 to 130 lines in about 900 human-written docs, and `actually`
# often marks a real contrast with what the reader expects (`the file
# is actually a symlink`). `very` skips a preceding `the`, `this`, or
# `that`, where it means exact (`the very first entry`). Weir has no
# lookbehind, so the filter consumes the word before and narrows the
# finding back to `very`.
expr main [genuinely, <(![the, this, that] very), very>, really, truly, actually]
let message "Intensifier. Give the number, or delete the word."
let description "Flags `genuinely`, `very`, `really`, `truly`, and `actually`, which usually raise a claim without informing."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The badge in the footer is genuine."
allows "The path is contended under load."
allows "The very first entry holds the header."
